// Command gen writes the review Skill from the Rules constant so the two never drift
package main

import (
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"

	"github.com/jeon-jihyeon/nodloop/internal/diagnose"
)

const skill = `---
name: review
description: Evidence-grounded review of an event from registered data and procedures with nodloop, with the user's verdict recorded as feedback. Use when the user asks to check, review or investigate metrics, anomalies or an incident, and when the user corrects a review that nodloop recorded.
---

# Review with nodloop

<!-- Generated by go generate from internal/diagnose/gen and the Rules constant in internal/diagnose/prompt.go. Edit those, not this file -->

## First setup

When a tool answers that no data directory is configured, ask the user for the directory that holds their events.csv, policy.yaml and procedures, run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop setup --data-dir <dir>`" + ` yourself and ask the user to reconnect the nodloop server. A user who wants to try nodloop first can clone https://github.com/jeon-jihyeon/nodloop and point setup at its ` + "`examples/demo`" + ` folder. When the server does not start because its config is broken, show the user the error it printed and run the same setup command. Records go to ` + "`~/.nodloop/records`" + ` unless the config or ` + "`NODLOOP_RECORD_DIR`" + ` names another directory. Setup keeps the record directory saved before when it gets no ` + "`--record-dir`" + ` and prints the data and the records in use. When setup refuses a broken config because it was given no ` + "`--record-dir`" + `, never rerun it with the default: ask the user which record directory they used and rerun with that ` + "`--record-dir`" + `, or fix the config.json it names. When setup warns that ` + "`NODLOOP_FILE_DIR`" + ` wins over the saved data directory, show the warning and tell the user to unset the variable where the nodloop server starts, because until then every review reads the directory it names. When it warns that the records hold the reviews and knowledge of another data directory, show the warning and ask the user whether to run setup again with a new ` + "`--record-dir`" + `, or to point ` + "`NODLOOP_RECORD_DIR`" + ` at a new directory when the warning names it, because otherwise corrections and knowledge from that data carry into reviews of the new one

## Steps

1. Find the event. Call ` + "`events`" + ` when the user names a period or a source instead of an event id
2. Call ` + "`observe`" + ` with the event id and read the observations. Numbers come from here, never from your own arithmetic
3. Call ` + "`context`" + ` with the event id. Keep the pending id. Read the knowledge candidates and the example candidates. When ` + "`candidates_omitted`" + ` is true the lists were cut at the cap, so tell the user that more candidates exist than were offered
4. Call ` + "`select`" + ` with the pending id and the candidates that apply to this event, one reason each. Call it with empty lists when none applies. Judge relevance yourself. Do not pick the newest few by habit and do not conclude that nothing applies before reading every candidate
5. Write the review as JSON that matches the schema in the context output, from the context text and the selected text. Follow the rules below. When you summarize selected knowledge keep its units, conditions and exceptions
6. Call ` + "`record`" + ` with the pending id and the review. When it answers with ` + "`recorded`" + ` false and ` + "`revise`" + ` reasons, fix only what the reasons name and call ` + "`record`" + ` again with the same pending id. Show the user the review that record returns, never the draft. record may turn the review into a hold
7. When the user corrects or approves the review, call ` + "`feedback`" + ` with the trace id that record returned, never the pending id, the verdict approve, edit or reject, the reason in the user's words and the corrected review in full when the verdict is edit. Pass ` + "`audit`" + ` true when the review was marked audit by ` + "`queue`" + `
8. After a correction that would help a later similar event, call ` + "`propose`" + ` with ` + "`from`" + ` set to the trace id that record returned, the kind, a meaning of the data or a judgment rule, and as content one sentence that states what the correction taught, written from the difference between the original and the corrected review. Code fills the scope from the change context and the metrics that moved and the evidence from that trace. Pass change contexts, metrics or dims such as platform ios only to change the scope it filled. Show the user the scope and the content that ` + "`propose`" + ` returns, say the content is your draft and tell the user it is a candidate until they approve it. Call ` + "`approve`" + ` only when the user says so and gives their name. When the correction forbids a tool call that its input alone decides, such as a shell command pattern or a file path, propose it as a judgment with ` + "`veto`" + `: the tool, the conditions on ` + "`tool_input`" + ` fields and an example input the veto must block. Show the user the veto that ` + "`propose`" + ` returns before they approve it. When ` + "`approve`" + ` answers ` + "`veto`" + ` true, tell the user the veto now sits in an approved veto file under ` + "`~/.claude/nodloop`" + ` and blocks the call only once the guard hook is registered, and offer to run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop guard install`" + ` for them. When it answers ` + "`veto_export_error`" + `, the approval is recorded but the veto file is not, so show the error and offer to run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop knowledge export`" + `. When it answers ` + "`folder_error`" + `, the approval is recorded but its folder could not be read, so tell the user the item is approved and show the error instead of the folder. ` + "`propose`" + ` answers the folder the item joins: the approved items a review would carry with it and their size against the budget. When ` + "`approve`" + ` fails because the folder may outgrow the review, show the user the items the error names and ask which way out to take: retire one, approve a new version of one that replaces it, or propose the new item with a narrower scope. Do only what the user picks. When ` + "`propose`" + ` or ` + "`approve`" + ` answers ` + "`compaction_due`" + ` true, tell the user the folder holds more than five approved items an event can replay and offer a compaction, which replaces them with fewer items that never overlap. Compact only when the user asks: call ` + "`compaction`" + ` with an item id of the folder, write the new items by the rules it returns, each naming the old items it replaces, show the user the draft and stop there. Items it lists as excluded cite only procedure paragraphs and stay as they are. Call ` + "`propose_compaction`" + ` with the draft only when the user asks for it after seeing the draft. When ` + "`compaction`" + ` answers ` + "`pending`" + ` or ` + "`propose_compaction`" + ` fails because another compaction of the same items is pending, name that compaction and offer to replay and approve it or to abandon it by retiring one of its candidates with ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop knowledge retire <id> --version <n> --approver <name>`" + `. ` + "`propose_compaction`" + ` answers the compaction id and the number of replay events. Tell the user that the replay reviews each event again with the new items, one or two model calls per event, and offer to run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop knowledge replay <compaction id>`" + ` through Bash. Call ` + "`approve_compaction`" + ` only after the replay passed and the user approves and names themselves. When it answers with events that failed, show them and offer to replay those events again with ` + "`--events`" + ` or to draft again
9. When the user reports what a real check found, call ` + "`outcome`" + ` with the trace id that record returned and confirmed, refuted or inconclusive
10. Call ` + "`detail`" + ` only when the user asks for the rows behind an observation
11. When the user asks what waits for a verdict, call ` + "`queue`" + ` and show the reviews in the order it returns with their reasons and which ones are marked audit. Call ` + "`pending`" + ` when the user asks about contexts that were built but never recorded
12. When the user asks how the knowledge holds up, call ` + "`knowledge_health`" + ` and show the retire candidates, the versions past their review deadline and the broken references. It only reads. Call ` + "`reaffirm`" + ` only when the user rechecked a version, explicitly reaffirms it and names themselves. For a version whose reviews were refuted, offer to run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop knowledge narrow <id> --version <n>`" + `, which proposes a version without the change contexts where it failed

## Rules

%s

## Output

- Show status, observations, causes with their paragraph ids, checks in order with their purpose, open questions and hold reasons
- Say that the review is recorded and give the trace id
- Never show a review that was not returned by record
`

func main() {
	os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:], os.Stderr))
}

// Returns the exit code so only main exits the process
func run(args []string, stderr io.Writer) int {
	flags := flag.NewFlagSet("gen", flag.ContinueOnError)
	flags.SetOutput(stderr)
	out := flags.String("out", "SKILL.md", "output path")
	if err := flags.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return 2
	}
	if err := write(*out); err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, "gen:", err)
		return 1
	}
	return 0
}

func write(out string) error {
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(out), 0o755); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	return os.WriteFile(out, []byte(fmt.Sprintf(skill, diagnose.Rules)), 0o644)
}
